  <static seed="125" source="Contrib/DMOI/1-Counting/1_3-Combinations_and_Permutations/1_3_12.pg">
    <statement><p>Consider sets <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> with <m>|A| = 14</m> and <m>|B| = 21\text{.}</m> 
<ol label="a.">
<li>
<p>How many functions <m>f: A \to B</m> are there?</p>
<p><fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>How many functions <m>f: A \to B</m> are injective?</p>
<p><fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>21^{14}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\mathop{\rm P}\nolimits\mathopen{}\left(21,14\right)</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p><ol label="a.">
<li>
<p><m>21^{14}</m> functions.  There are 21 choices for the image of each element in the domain.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>P(21, 14)=21\cdot20\cdot19\cdot \dots \cdot 7</m> injective functions.  There are 21  choices for the image of the first element of the domain, then only 20 choices for the second, 19 for the third, and so on.</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</solution>
